label Day1
\Sylvia slides the fuller teacup onto my mat and keeps the one with the small chip on the rim for herself. #id:line.Day1.one
\“The tables by the door are in a draught,” she says. “You always complain about your throat when you sit under the vent.” #id:line.Day1.002
\“And the window light happens to be behind your chair.” #id:line.Day1.003
\“Someone had to face the glare.” #id:line.Day1.004
\She holds her phone out at arm's length, then lowers it half an inch without taking the shot. #id:line.Day1.005
\“Wait. Your left collar is turned under,” she says. “Don't move.” #id:line.Day1.006
\Her free hand reaches across the corner of the table. Two cool fingers slip beneath the lapel of my blouse, smooth the linen flat against the collarbone, and withdraw before the cloth can warm them. #id:line.Day1.007
\The chair legs scrape half an inch closer until both faces fit inside the screen. #id:line.Day1.008
\“Not that expression,” she says. #id:line.Day1.009
\“It's my expression.” #id:line.Day1.010
\“It's the one you save for university circulars.” #id:line.Day1.011
\A slight widening of the mouth. #id:line.Day1.012
\“Worse.” #id:line.Day1.013
\On the table between the saucers, my own phone sounds. #id:line.Day1.014
\Lavinia, message\: Try not to look too pleased when you see me tomorrow. #id:line.Day1.015
\Eleven words. No greeting, and tomorrow at the end. #id:line.Day1.016
\Above the keyboard bar, a small grey line appears\: Lavinia is recording an audio message... #id:line.Day1.017
\The three dots pulse between the cups for a few seconds, then vanish without sending, leaving the bar blank. #id:line.Day1.018
\Sylvia takes the photograph. #id:line.Day1.019
\“Oh,” she says, lowering the phone. “You were looking down. Shall I delete that one for you?” #id:line.Day1.020
\“Let me see it first.” #id:line.Day1.021
\She brings her phone between us. In the picture, the gaze is dropped slightly below the lens—still toward the table where the message lit—while Sylvia has caught herself watching the side of my face. #id:line.Day1.022
\“Who was it?” #id:line.Day1.023
\“Lavinia.” #id:line.Day1.024
\“I should have asked her to help earlier.” #id:line.Day1.025
\Two fingers spread across the glass to enlarge the picture. Sylvia leans against my arm to see what is being inspected. #id:line.Day1.026
\“Don't inspect it that closely.” #id:line.Day1.027
\“I like it.” #id:line.Day1.028
\“That isn't usually why you zoom in.” #id:line.Day1.029
\Her face in this one, too. #id:line.Day1.030
\Ask for another photograph. #id:line.Day1.031
label Day1.thought
return

label Day1.photo.yes
\“Wait. One more.” #id:line.Day1.photo.yes.001
\“We have a good one.” #id:line.Day1.photo.yes.002
\“You aren't looking at the camera.” #id:line.Day1.photo.yes.003
\“I was supervising.” #id:line.Day1.photo.yes.004
\The chair pulls closer; this time the eyes stay on the screen instead of my phone. #id:line.Day1.photo.yes.005
\Sylvia tries to arrange her mouth, looks across the screen, and laughs before she takes the picture. #id:line.Day1.photo.yes.006
\“Now you aren't looking at the camera either,” she says. #id:line.Day1.photo.yes.007
\“Send me both.” #id:line.Day1.photo.yes.008
\She does. #id:line.Day1.photo.yes.009
