label Day1.reading
\Sylvia puts her phone away and moves her chair to the other side of the table. #id:line.Day1.reading.001
\The paperback beneath Sylvia's bag has a bent cover. She smooths it with her palm. #id:line.Day1.reading.002
\The Funeral Rehearsal. #id:line.Day1.reading.003
\“She insists on attending,” Sylvia says. #id:line.Day1.reading.004
\“Her own?” #id:line.Day1.reading.005
\“Listen.” #id:line.Day1.reading.006
\She opens it near the beginning. #id:line.Day1.reading.007
\“‘The eulogy was excellent. She asked whether it could be about her.’” #id:line.Day1.reading.008
\A laugh escapes before the cup reaches the saucer. Sylvia's finger stays beneath the sentence. #id:line.Day1.reading.009
\“I read that standing in the shop.” #id:line.Day1.reading.010
\“You bought a whole novel for one line?” #id:line.Day1.reading.011
\“I wanted to know what she'd object to next.” #id:line.Day1.reading.012
\The corner of the next page lifts under her thumb. #id:line.Day1.reading.013
\Sylvia lowers her voice. #id:line.Day1.reading.014
\“‘Again,’ said the aunt. ‘You sound relieved.’” #id:line.Day1.reading.015
\“Is she in the coffin?” #id:line.Day1.reading.016
\“An armchair.” #id:line.Day1.reading.017
\“More convenient for supervising.” #id:line.Day1.reading.018
\Sylvia finds the next speech. #id:line.Day1.reading.019
\“There's a man at the door. He says he's come about a carriage.” #id:line.Day1.reading.020
\Sylvia puts one hand against her chest. #id:line.Day1.reading.021
\“‘My cousin required the carriage.’” #id:line.Day1.reading.022
\A laugh catches before she has finished the last word. #id:line.Day1.reading.023
\“What?” #id:line.Day1.reading.024
\“The voice.” #id:line.Day1.reading.025
\“He's in love.” #id:line.Day1.reading.026
\“Give me his part.” #id:line.Day1.reading.027
\She turns the book toward me, keeping a finger beneath the line. I read his next speech in the same voice. #id:line.Day1.reading.028
\Sylvia bends over the table. A moment passes before she can stop laughing. #id:line.Day1.reading.029
\“Too pleased with himself,” she says. “He thinks this is working.” #id:line.Day1.reading.030
\The line repeats without the flourish on the last word. #id:line.Day1.reading.031
\“Yes. Like that.” #id:line.Day1.reading.032
\She takes the book back. #id:line.Day1.reading.033
\For a while, we read in turns. Sylvia gives the aunt the careful, carrying voice of someone repeating a reasonable request. When I try to do her as well, Sylvia kicks the leg of my chair. #id:line.Day1.reading.034
\“Mine.” #id:line.Day1.reading.035
\“All right.” #id:line.Day1.reading.036
\The aunt again. Before he can finish. #id:line.Day1.reading.037
\The phone lights up on the table. #id:line.Day1.reading.038
\Lavinia, message\: Are you busy? #id:line.Day1.reading.039
\Priscilla, message\: Reading with Sylvia. #id:line.Day1.reading.040
\Lavinia, message\: Anything good? #id:line.Day1.reading.041
\Priscilla, message\: She's making it better. #id:line.Day1.reading.042
\“Should I send it to you?” Sylvia asks. #id:line.Day1.reading.043
\“What?” #id:line.Day1.reading.044
\“My part. You're answering those quite quickly.” #id:line.Day1.reading.045
\The message goes through; the phone returns to the wood. #id:line.Day1.reading.046
\“Sorry. Where are we?” #id:line.Day1.reading.047
\She puts her finger beneath the line. #id:line.Day1.reading.048
\One exchange, then another, before a hand stops her at the margin. #id:line.Day1.reading.049
